This is a study running a maximum loading case on the spoiler for a 100 year storm gust, with a flow of 59.5m/s at the turbine height.
The wind was only tested in a head on flow, so the maximum Z component of the force would still need to be tested, and the spoiler was modeled as a closed surface for ease of modeling (this has shown to not hugely impact the force calculations in previous studies. See shellCases folder)
The building modeled was a simple block with a spoiler running the length of the building. This was constructed using the aspectRatio code, and was chosen because it was the building dimensions that displayed the maximum speedup and thus could be used to find the maximum loading case.
The expansion layers didn't form wonderfully due to the high velocities, and the yPlus folder in postProcessing or visualizations in paraView can be checked to show specifics 
The average ground y+ value was ~1000, the building was ~100, and the spoiler itself was ~25. This means that the results might not be the most precise, but they do agree with trends found in previous studies (more information on the Maximum Loading Case Coda page in CFD > Aspect Ratio)
The model had an upped refinement level and complete layer formation (can check in log/mpiExecMesh for specifics) but even with a cell count of over 75 million, it wasn't enough to achieve acceptable y+ values
For future re-runs of the model, potential fixes to this would be modifying the snappyHexMesh file, making the firstLayerThickness smaller, the finalLayerThickness larger, and increasing the number of layers for each surface past 25 (although beware that OpenFOAM rarely likes more than 25 inflation layers)
There also is a Pressure Map of the Spoiler included as a csv file named "spoilerMaxPressureMap" that includes both the pressure and wallShearStress at each point, which can be used for more exact calculations.